Q1. What is the Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)?
Why do we need it?

Interview Ready Answer

The Single Responsibility Principle (SRP) states that

"A class should have only one responsibility
or one reason to change."

It means a class should perform only one specific task.
If a class has multiple unrelated responsibilities,
any change in one responsibility may affect the others.

Why do we need SRP?

• Improves Maintainability.
• Reduces Code Duplication.
• Improves Readability.
• Makes Testing Easier.
• Reduces Coupling.
• Makes code easier to extend.

Real-Life Example

Restaurant

Chef
↓

Only cooks food.

Cashier
↓

Only handles billing.

Cleaner
↓

Only cleans.

Each person has one responsibility.

======================================================================

Q2. Can a class have multiple methods and still
follow SRP?

Interview Ready Answer

Yes.

A class can have multiple methods as long as
all methods belong to the same responsibility.

SRP is about

"One Responsibility"

NOT

"One Method"

Example

class BankAccount{

    void deposit(){}

    void withdraw(){}

    double getBalance(){

        return 0;
    }
}

All methods are related to

Bank Account Management.

Therefore,

SRP is NOT violated.

------------------------------------------------------------

Example (Violates SRP)

class UserService{

    void registerUser(){}

    void sendEmail(){}

    void generateReport(){}

    void saveToDatabase(){}
}

This class has multiple unrelated responsibilities.

======================================================================

Q3. How do you identify that a class is violating SRP
in a real project?

Interview Ready Answer

A class is likely violating SRP if

• It performs multiple unrelated tasks.
• It has more than one reason to change.
• Different developers modify it for different reasons.
• It becomes too large.
• It becomes difficult to test.
• It becomes difficult to maintain.

Example

class UserService{

    registerUser();

    sendEmail();

    generateReport();

    saveToDatabase();
}

This class has

• Registration Responsibility

• Email Responsibility

• Report Responsibility

• Database Responsibility

Therefore,

It violates SRP.

======================================================================

Q4. Does SRP improve Maintainability and Testability?

Interview Ready Answer

Yes.

SRP improves Maintainability because

• Bugs are easier to locate.
• Changes are isolated.
• Code becomes easier to understand.
• Only one class needs modification.

SRP improves Testability because

• Each class can be tested independently.
• Unit testing becomes easier.
• Less mocking is required.
• Fewer side effects occur.

Real Project Example

Without SRP

class UserService{

    registerUser();

    sendEmail();

    generateReport();

    saveToDatabase();
}

If email functionality changes,

UserService must be modified,
which increases the risk of affecting
registration or report generation.

------------------------------------------------------------

With SRP

UserService

↓

Handles User Registration

EmailService

↓

Handles Email Sending

ReportService

↓

Generates Reports

UserRepository

↓

Stores User Data

Now

• Email changes only affect EmailService.
• Database changes only affect UserRepository.
• Each class can be tested independently.

